course: dictionary
lesson: 1160
title: Context clues from a French word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word you do not know often has clues in the words around it. Read the sentence first, then look for clues. «In the south of France people often eat le gâteau des rois instead, a brioche ring with candied fruit, which also hides a fève.» The word instead tells you the cake is a swap for something, and the words ring and candied fruit tell you what a brioche looks like and what it has in it.
explain: Now test your idea. Swap in a meaning: a brioche is a soft, sweet bread, so «a soft, sweet bread ring with candied fruit» still makes sense. If your swapped meaning makes sense, you are probably right. If it does not, read the sentence again.
explain: Brioche is a French word that English kept. You may see it on a menu, in a bakery, or in a history book. When a word comes from another language, the clues are often the same as in English: look at the words around it, and check a dictionary if you are still unsure.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Brioche is a noun. You can say a brioche, or some brioche.
fact: «Brioche» means a soft, sweet French bread made with eggs and butter, often shaped like a bun with a little top knot.
fact: «Brioche» is a French word used in English, so it is kept in its French spelling.
fact: Phrases you may meet: «a slice of brioche» and «brioche bun».
exercise: What does «brioche» mean? | *A soft, sweet French bread made with eggs and butter | A spicy sausage | A hard, dry biscuit from Scotland | A fizzy drink
exercise: In the sentence «a brioche ring with candied fruit», what kind of thing is a brioche? | A kind of tree | A type of fruit | A town in France | *A bread or cake ring
exercise: Why is «brioche» spelled and said the French way in English? | It means bread in Scots | *It is a French word kept in English | It is a Scottish word | It is a made-up word
test: Which sentence uses «brioche» correctly? | Brioche the big red bus went home. | The brioches was very happy. | She brioched the window quickly. | *She ate a slice of brioche with her tea.
test: In «le gâteau des rois», what is eaten in the south of France? | A type of cheese | A cold soup | *A brioche ring with candied fruit | A fizzy French drink
exam: A famous line is «qu'ils mangent de la brioche», French for a phrase about bread and cake. What is brioche? | A kind of wine | A type of cake with icing | A small French village | *A soft, sweet French bread
